# Frequent commits (this repo)

Feature work on `hyku-oob` (and branches cut from it) may use multiple small commits per PR.

## Override for this workspace

**Do not wait** for an explicit “commit” request. Commit when it is appropriate (below), unless the user has said to hold, leave uncommitted, or only stage.

Still follow git safety (no force-push, no secrets, no `--no-verify`, amend only under the usual strict amend rules). **Never push** unless the user asks in that turn ([no-git-push](./no-git-push.mdc)).

## When to commit (proactively)

Commit after a **coherent, reviewable slice** that leaves the tree in a sensible state, for example:

- A feature vertical slice (service + wiring + specs) that is green for what it touches
- A deliberate UX / copy / product-framing pass
- A quality / DRY / naming pass after review
- Finishing one acceptance chunk before starting the next
- Before a risky refactor, so rollback is easy

Prefer **several focused commits** over one large dump at the end of a long session.

## When not to commit yet

- Broken or half-migrated code you are still mid-edit
- Red `bundle exec rspec` / `bundle exec rubocop` for Ruby work you are claiming done in that commit ([code-quality-standards](./code-quality-standards.mdc), [general-instructions](./general-instructions.mdc))
- Unrelated dirty files (e.g. local env overrides) — leave them out
- User explicitly asked to pause committing

## How

- Use the normal commit HEREDOC flow and message style for this repo (why over what).
- You may tell the user briefly that you committed (hash + one line); do not ask permission first when the criteria above are met.
